# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) and now also runs on
# Linux and macOS. Without these rules, git hands a Linux user CRLF copies of
# the shell scripts, and `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` fails with the famously
# unhelpful "bad interpreter: No such file or directory".
#
# Files that a POSIX system executes or parses must be LF in the working tree,
# on every platform. Files that cmd.exe parses must be CRLF — a .bat with LF
# endings is mis-tokenised by cmd, which produces equally confusing errors.

# Default: normalise to LF in the repository, let git decide in the checkout.
* text=auto

# ── Must be LF everywhere ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# ── Must be CRLF everywhere ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# ── Binary: never touch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.obj           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.faiss         binary
*.db            binary
